"], tests = ?. Let's simulate ddmin quickly.\n\nWe'll assume the algorithm from Zeller's paper: ddmin(F) where F is list, and test function returns True if failure (i.e., set contains all required_difference). We'll implement a typical version:\n\ndef ddmin(F):\n    n = 2\n    result = F[:]\n    while len(result) > 1:\n        # try subsets\n        subset_size = len(result) // n\n        found = False\n        for i in range(n):\n            subset = result[i*subset_size : (i+1)*subset_size]\n            if test(subset):\n                result = subset\n                found = True\n                break\n        if found:\n            n = 2\n            continue\n        # try complements\n        for i in range(n):\n            complement = result[:i*subset_size] + result[(i+1)*subset_size:]\n            if test(complement):\n                result = complement\n                found = True\n                break\n        if found:\n            n = 2\n            continue\n        # increase granularity\n        n = min(n*2, len(result))\n    return result\n\nBut the exact counting of tests: each call to test is a test. We need to count total tests performed. This algorithm may not precisely match the expected because the order of subsets and complements depends on the specific implementation.\n\nHowever, given that the fixtures are provided, maybe we can deduce the exact number of tests for case 1. Let's simulate manually.\n\nF = [c0,c1,c2,x0,x1,x2,x3,x4,x5] (9 elements). test(set) returns True iff set contains both x4 and x5.\n\nStep 1: n=2, subset_size = 9//2 = 4 (integer division, floor). Two subsets: first 4: [c0,c1,c2,x0] does it contain both x4 and x5? No. test returns False. second subset: [x1,x2,x3,x4] contains x4 but not x5 -> False. So no subset fails. Then try complements: complement of first subset is [x1,x2,x3,x4,x5] which contains both x4,x5 -> True. So found complement. result becomes complement = [x1,x2,x3,x4,x5]. n resets to 2. Test count: 2 su